  "passage": {
    "title": "The Northern Lights",
    "paragraphs": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "The northern lights, also called the aurora borealis, are one of nature's most beautiful shows. They appear as green, pink or purple lights that move slowly across the night sky. People have admired them for thousands of years, and many cultures have stories to explain them."
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "The lights are caused by the sun. The sun sends out a stream of tiny particles, and when these reach the Earth, they meet its magnetic field. Most of the particles are pushed away, but some travel along the field lines towards the north and south poles. There they hit gases in the air, and the gases give off light. Oxygen produces the green and red colours, while nitrogen produces blue and purple."
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "The best places to see the northern lights are close to the Arctic Circle: northern Norway, Sweden, Finland, Iceland and parts of Canada and Alaska. The lights are most active between September and March, when the nights are long and dark. You need a clear sky, so a cloudy night is a disappointment, and it is better to be far from city lights. Many tourists join organised trips, which cost from three hundred to over a thousand pounds, but local guides say the best chance is simply to be patient."
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Scientists can now predict when the lights will be strong, because they watch the sun for storms. A large solar storm can make the aurora visible much farther south than usual. In 2024, people saw it in countries such as Italy and Mexico. However, strong storms also cause problems for satellites and electricity networks, so forecasts are important for more than tourism."
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "If you plan to go, remember to dress warmly. Waiting outside at night in winter can be very cold, and you may wait for hours. A phone with a night-photo setting or a camera with a tripod will help you keep a memory of what you see."
      }
    ]
  },
